Let me analyze this PR carefully.

The key concern is in `makeManual.mts`. The `testName` argument is used both as a filename (in `gen` directory) and pushed to `manualTests.txt`. But in `convertFourslash.mts`, the manual tests set is checked against `testName` which is derived from `test.name` with first letter uppercased, not the file name.

Let me look at the mismatch:

In `convertFourslash.mts`:
```js
const testName = test.name[0].toUpperCase() + test.name.substring(1);
if (manualTests.has(testName)) {
    return;
}
```

The test file is written as `${test.name}_test.go`. So the file is `foo_test.go` but manualTests must contain the uppercased Go test function name (e.g. `Foo`).

In `makeManual.mts`, `testName` is `args[0]` which is used directly as the filename in `gen` dir: `path.join(genDir, ${testFileName})`. So the user passes the full filename like `foo_test.go`. That gets pushed to manualTests.txt as `foo_test.go`.

This is a real mismatch: `convertFourslash` checks `manualTests.has("Foo")` but `makeManual` writes `"foo_test.go"` to the file. These will never match.

Let me confirm and report.

```json
{
  "comments": [
    {
      "path": "internal/fourslash/_scripts/makeManual.mts",
      "start_line": 15,
      "end_line": 38,
      "content": "The value written to `manualTests.txt` is inconsistent with the value checked in `convertFourslash.mts`. Here `testName` is the CLI argument used as a filename (e.g. `foo_test.go`), and that raw string is pushed into `manualTests.txt`. However, `convertFourslash.mts` checks membership using `const testName = test.name[0].toUpperCase() + test.name.substring(1)` (e.g. `Foo`). These will never match, so tests moved to `manual` will be regenerated in `gen` on the next `convertfourslash` run. Normalize both sides to the same identifier (e.g. derive the capitalized `test.name` from the filename before writing, or have convertFourslash compare against the file basename)."
    },
    {
      "path": "internal/fourslash/_scripts/makeManual.mts",
      "start_line": 10,
      "end_line": 12,
      "content": "When `args.length === 0`, the script calls `process.exit(1)` without printing any usage/error message, making misuse hard to diagnose. Consider logging a usage message (e.g. the expected `<testFileName>` argument) before exiting."
    }
  ]
}
```
